  instructions: [
    'Chill everything. Place your mixing bowl, ground beef, and jerky-gun barrel in the fridge for 30 minutes. Cold meat extrudes cleanly; warm meat smears.',
    'Mix the cure and dry spices thoroughly into the ground beef by hand for 3–5 minutes until the meat turns visibly tacky. This protein extraction is what holds the strips together after drying.',
    'Add soy sauce, Worcestershire, liquid smoke (if using), and brown sugar. Mix another 2 minutes. Cover and refrigerate 12–24 hours.',
    'Load the jerky-gun barrel gradually — push the plunger fully forward, pull back two inches, pack meat into that space, pull back another two inches, pack again. This eliminates air pockets that cause blowouts.',
    'Extrude onto dehydrator trays at a 45° angle, moving the gun backward at a steady pace. Aim for strips about 1/8 inch thick and 5–6 inches long.',
    'Dehydrate at 160°F for 4–6 hours until the jerky bends and cracks along the fibers without snapping. Blot excess fat with paper towels while warm, then cool completely before storing.',
  ],
};

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'What is a jerky gun?',
    answer: 'A jerky gun is a caulking-gun-shaped kitchen tool that presses seasoned ground meat through a nozzle into uniform strips or round sticks. You load the barrel with 1–1.5 lb of cold ground beef, venison, or turkey, attach a flat or round nozzle, and squeeze the trigger to extrude perfectly even jerky pieces straight onto a dehydrator tray — no slicing required.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How do you use a jerky gun?',
    answer: 'Load 1–1.5 lb of chilled, seasoned ground meat into the barrel in small increments — push the plunger all the way forward, pull back two inches, pack meat into the space, and repeat. Attach your nozzle (single flat for standard strips), hold the gun at a 45° angle over a dehydrator tray, squeeze the trigger with steady pressure while moving backward, and cut strips to length with kitchen shears.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the best jerky gun brand?',
    answer: 'The LEM Jerky Cannon and Weston jerky guns are the two most durable choices — both use anodized aluminum barrels that outlast plastic models. LEM ships with two nozzles and Backwoods seasoning included. Hi Mountain and Nesco are cheaper and lighter but rely more on plastic parts. For occasional use, any of the four work; for weekly batches, spring for LEM or Weston.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Do you need cure with a jerky gun?',
    answer: 'Yes — curing salt (Prague Powder #1) is strongly recommended for any ground-meat jerky. Grinding exposes more surface area to bacteria than sliced whole-muscle meat, and the long low-temperature drying keeps the meat in the food-safety danger zone for hours. Use exactly 1/4 teaspoon per pound. The USDA recommends dehydrating ground-meat jerky at 160°F to eliminate pathogens; cure adds a second layer of protection against botulism.',
  },
  {
    question: "What's the difference between a jerky gun and a jerky cannon?",
    answer: 'None — they are the same tool with different brand names. "Jerky Cannon" is LEM Products\' trademarked name for their model; "jerky gun" is the generic term everyone else uses. Both are ground-meat extruders with the same barrel-and-plunger design. Hi Mountain, Nesco, Weston, and Smokehouse all call theirs a jerky gun. Chard uses "jerky shooter." Same tool, different marketing.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can you use ground beef in a jerky gun?',
    answer: 'Yes — ground beef is what jerky guns are built for. Use 90/10 lean or leaner (93/7 and 95/5 work even better). Fatter blends cause greasy jerky and shorter shelf life because the fat melts, seeps out, and eventually goes rancid. If your grocery store only carries 80/20, you can rinse it or ask the butcher to grind a lean roast (top round or eye of round) to order.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How do you clean a jerky gun?',
    answer: 'Disassemble the gun immediately after use — separate the barrel, plunger, nozzles, and retaining ring. Wash each part in hot soapy water with the included cleaning brush, paying special attention to the nozzle threads where meat tends to trap. Most aluminum barrels are hand-wash only (dishwasher heat can warp gaskets). Dry every part fully before reassembling — trapped moisture leads to pitting and mold.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can you use a caulking gun as a jerky gun?',
    answer: 'A construction caulking gun is not food-safe out of the box — the frame, plunger, and springs are treated with industrial oils and lubricants that should never touch meat. Some hobbyists convert Milwaukee-style guns using food-grade sausage tubes as inserts, but the setup takes work and still leaves the metal parts to sanitize. For under $30, a real jerky gun (Nesco, Hi Mountain) removes every one of those problems.',
  },
];

const relatedLinks = [
