  <h2 id="ground-beef-jerky-gun-recipe">Ground Beef Jerky Gun Recipe (Variations)</h2>
  <p>The recipe card above is the base — it works for any ground meat that hits 90/10 lean. Here's how to swing the flavor without changing the ratios:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Teriyaki:</strong> Swap Worcestershire for pineapple juice; add 1 tsp fresh grated ginger and 1 tsp sesame oil.</li>
    <li><strong>Spicy:</strong> Add 1 tsp cayenne, 1 tsp red-pepper flakes, and 2 tsp sriracha (subtract 1 tbsp soy so it doesn't over-salt).</li>
    <li><strong>Sweet-and-hot:</strong> Double the brown sugar to 2 tbsp; add 1/2 tsp cayenne.</li>
    <li><strong>BBQ:</strong> Add 2 tbsp of your favorite Kansas City-style BBQ sauce; subtract 1 tbsp soy.</li>
    <li><strong>Hamburger jerky:</strong> Same recipe, but skip the cure only if you're eating within 3 days and refrigerating. Otherwise cure it — hamburger jerky and ground-beef jerky are the same product with different marketing.</li>
  </ul>

  <ContentImage src="/images/jerky-gun-nozzles.webp" alt="Three jerky gun nozzles side by side — single flat, double flat, and round snack stick — with the extruded meat shapes each one produces" />

  <h2 id="deer-jerky-gun">Deer Jerky Gun (Venison & Wild Game)</h2>
  <p>The jerky gun is the reason ground-venison jerky exists. Wild venison is naturally 92–95% lean, which is above the 90/10 minimum, so it hits the barrel already in the sweet spot. The only adjustment: add 1 tbsp beef tallow or bacon fat per pound to keep the finished jerky from being too dry — venison has almost no marbling, and the little bit of fat you add back in makes a huge texture difference.</p>

  <p>Same rules apply for elk, moose, and antelope: 90/10 or leaner base + a tablespoon of added beef fat + Prague Powder #1 + the recipe above. Do not use trimmings from a road-kill or improperly-field-dressed animal in ground jerky — the surface-area exposure is why cure matters more here than on any other meat. See <a href="/venison-jerky/">our venison jerky guide</a> for whole-muscle instructions if you'd rather slice than extrude.</p>

  <h2 id="jerky-gun-tips">Jerky Gun Tips</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Use the single flat nozzle, not the double.</strong> The double nozzle sounds efficient — twice the strips per pull — but the pressure distributes unevenly between the two openings. You get one thick strip and one skinny one, and they dry at different rates. Single flat every time.</li>
    <li><strong>Cold meat, cold gun.</strong> Both go in the fridge for 30 minutes before you start. Warm meat is the number-one cause of a jerky gun that "won't work."</li>
    <li><strong>Do not over-tighten the retaining ring.</strong> Half a turn past hand-tight is enough. Any more and you'll fight the ring for five minutes when you try to clean up.</li>
    <li><strong>Wipe the plunger with a drop of vegetable oil before loading.</strong> Not sunflower — its taste can carry through. Neutral vegetable oil on the rubber gasket makes the plunger glide, and the trigger takes half the effort.</li>
    <li><strong>Cut strips with kitchen shears mid-extrusion, not with a knife after.</strong> Shears leave a clean edge; a knife drags the meat and rounds the ends.</li>
    <li><strong>Clean the gun within an hour of using it.</strong> Dried meat inside the nozzle threads becomes concrete. Hot water and the wire brush in the first hour = two-minute cleanup. Waiting until tomorrow = twenty minutes and some resentment.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="diy-jerky-gun">DIY Jerky Gun (and the Milwaukee Caulking Gun Question)</h2>
  <p>Every few months someone asks me if they can use a Milwaukee (or Ryobi, or DeWalt) caulking gun as a jerky gun. The short answer is no — not straight out of the box. Construction caulking guns are lubricated with industrial oils, the frames are painted with coatings not rated for food contact, and the springs are steel that will rust the first time it meets brine.</p>

  <p>Where the hack works: some people slide a food-grade sausage-stuffer tube inside the caulking-gun frame, load the meat into the tube, and use the tube as an insert. The caulking gun just provides the pressure. That's fine — but by the time you've bought the tube ($15), a food-grade nozzle ($8), and the specific caulking gun ($35 for a decent one), you've spent $58 on a rig that a $25 Nesco jerky gun does out of the box. Save yourself the engineering project.</p>

  <p>The one legitimate reason to consider a Milwaukee-frame conversion: you already own the caulking gun for construction, and you want to make jerky twice a year without buying a dedicated tool. In that specific case, buy a Weston 1-lb sausage-stuffer tube kit (labeled food-grade), pop it into the frame, and treat the tube as your barrel. Wash the tube like a jerky gun; never touch the caulking-gun frame to raw meat.</p>

  <ContentImage src="/images/jerky-gun-finished-strips.webp" alt="Finished ground beef jerky strips cooling on a wire rack after being extruded through a jerky gun and dehydrated at 160°F" />

  <h2 id="where-to-buy-jerky-gun">Where to Buy a Jerky Gun</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cabela's / Bass Pro:</strong> In-store pickup, full accessory aisle. Best if you want to hold it before buying.</li>
    <li><strong>Tractor Supply:</strong> Carries Weston and their house brand year-round; expands to LEM during holiday season.</li>
    <li><strong>Walmart:</strong> Nesco and Chard on the shelf; LEM and Weston on Walmart.com but not usually in stores.</li>
    <li><strong>Amazon:</strong> Every brand, fastest delivery, most reviews to compare. Watch out for third-party sellers on obscure brands.</li>
    <li><strong>Direct from manufacturer (lemproducts.com, westonbrands.com, himtnjerky.com):</strong> Best for warranty registration and buying replacement parts (plunger seals, gaskets) individually.</li>
  </ul>

  <ContentImage src="/images/jerky-gun-brands-lineup.webp" alt="Five popular jerky gun models lined up side by side — LEM Cannon, Weston, Nesco, Hi Mountain, and Chard — for size comparison" />

  <p>Whichever gun you buy, pair it with the right dehydrator — our <a href="/best-dehydrator-for-jerky/">best dehydrator for jerky guide</a> covers what to look for. And once you're extruding strips, <a href="/beef-jerky-seasoning/">the seasoning guide</a> walks through every base blend that pairs cleanly with ground-meat jerky.</p>
</PillarLayout>
